  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Nature and Mechanism
    
    A Nightshelf recursive ice-magic that fractures time and memory within frozen water. The Spiral manifests not as a crack but as a *recursion* — a spiraling pattern in which the same moment repeats in tighter and tighter rings, each layer holding a different version of what occurred. A practitioner presses flipper to ice and seals a moment inside it: a conversation, a scream, the exact angle of light when a rival fell. The ice remembers perfectly. The formations the Spiral produces do not decay. Recovered formations are subjective — a single specimen retrieved near the Glow-Fissure, a deep fissure bleeding taint into both penguin civilizations, held a sealed scream that different listeners identified as belonging to different individuals.
    
    The Spiral also resists systematic study. Operatives who attempted to map its parameters were subsequently unable to remember their findings — an effect distinct from the memory-bleed practitioners experience, and one I have encountered no satisfying explanation for in the deep ice or anywhere else.
    
    ## Cost and Training
    
    Every sealed moment bleeds back into the caster's own mind until the practitioner cannot distinguish personal memory from trapped recollection. Practitioners describe memories that are not theirs cycling back at wrong moments — each iteration leaving them a little less themselves, their eyes holding gaps where certainty used to be.
    
    Apprentices undergo physical scarification, cutting the Spiral's geometric patterns into their own flesh to forge a reading-bond with the ice. They bleed into it, and the ice reads them back. The bond is permanent. The cost is cumulative. Of the five practitioners known to have attempted mastery by the Faction Wars, three succeeded. Two survived the war. None survived themselves — a formulation the Nightshelf sources repeat with the specificity of someone who has counted carefully.
    
    ## Discovery and Lineage
    
    Umbra-Princeps Alnae~ Oss, a Nightshelf leader and practitioner of deep-ice magic, discovered the Spiral near The Veil Vents, a corruption source in The Berg's remote reaches, during the Great Thaw. Alnae~ Oss taught the art to Shadow-Broker Nyla, the former coordinator of Nightfall Shelf's shadow economy, who transmitted it to Augur ☽'Silvsiaelrous~ the Resolute, a crystalline-weaver of the ☽'shadow Covens' Order, and to Veil-render Seciae~. The lineage is unusually well-documented for Nightshelf practice — a consequence, I suspect, of the art's lethality making its chain of transmission a matter of institutional concern rather than personal discretion.
    
    ## Applications
    
    Beyond sealing memories, the Spiral enables practitioners to read frozen memory-scars in existing ice formations — parsing structural weaknesses, defensive wards, and architectural secrets invisible to those who lack the art.
    
    The Spiral's geometric patterns can develop gaps: widening breaks in the recursive sequence that function as conduits for prophetic vision. These gaps grow more pronounced during the Long Dark, when the boundary between living world and ice-memory thins. Practitioners who trace the patterns in ash rather than ice report the breaks widening over successive iterations.
    
    The Spiral can also be performed in reverse — its words spoken backward to *unmind* mastered knowledge and return it to the ice, where surrendered knowledge functions as a binding seal against artifacts of deep power. Alnae~ Oss first practiced this reversed form against the Omega Obelisk, a column of whale-bone carrying orca theology. ☽'Saetacaeia-Viamana~, a Communion mystic taught by Alnae~ Oss, later performed the reversed Spiral against the same artifact, sacrificing her mastery of three forbidden magics to partially bind it.
    
    ## Notable Deployments
    
    The Vum∴tenebra — the first confirmed internal deployment of the Spiral against a Nightshelf-aligned population — represents the art's most extensively documented use. The ☽'shadow Covens' Order's augurs read weaknesses in E'frostling defenses at Terrace Momiou, an Aurora-affiliated settlement, exploiting structural secrets in ice that had stood since before the Thaw. The Spiral also froze entire corridors mid-collapse, flooding lower retreat chambers. Seventeen died. The fire-deaths were *preserved* — every scream sealed in ice, every agony recycled through the memory-structures until the victims burned again and again across seasons. The ice at Terrace Momiou still holds those trapped penguins' confusion, their last thoughts cycling endlessly. I have felt similar impressions in Foundation strata. They do not diminish with age.
    
    During the breach at The Fissured Smelt~, a Nightshelf outpost, an unnamed practitioner used the Spiral to seal the chronicle of what happened — sealing her own death alongside the history. She became what the ice keeps: memory without self, chronicle without chronicler.
    
    ## Involuntary Manifestation
    
    The Spiral can emerge without deliberate invocation. A Nightshelf lector's obsessive circular polishing of the Thaw-Paths Medallion, an aurora-glass artifact of deep-ice resonance enshrined in ☽'secret Sanctninicaesis~, reportedly crossed from mundane repetition into the Spiral's recursive fracturing — the small circles of keeper-oil and chamois becoming the magic's concentric rings. The implication — that sufficient repetitive contact with ice of deep memory can trigger the Spiral unbidden — has not been systematically investigated, for reasons noted above.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771575610661_0", anchor: "The lineage is unusually well-documented for Nightshelf practice", text: "Unusually well-documented, and running through figures whose own chronologies I cannot verify. I have already flagged — on Nyla's entry and on Seciae~'s — that the former's \"thirty winters\" is self-reported accounting and the latter's dates contradict each other across strata. The most meticulously recorded transmission lineage in Nightshelf magic passes through two practitioners whose timelines dissolve under scrutiny. The chain is clear. The links are not.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771575610661_1", anchor: "Seventeen died", text: "Seventeen. Precise. From a corridor-flooding event during active combat, reported by the faction that caused the deaths. The Nightshelf counted their own kills with the care of accountants filing quarterly returns.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771575610661_2", anchor: "Operatives who attempted to map its parameters were subsequently unable to remember their findings", text: "A magic that eats the studies conducted on it. I wrote this sentence, set down my stylus, and stared at it for longer than professional detachment should permit. The Spiral does not merely resist classification — it removes the classifier's capacity to classify, through a mechanism distinct from its known costs. Every methodological framework I could bring to bear on this presupposes that the investigator retains their findings. The deep ice offers no explanation. The deep ice may have offered one that I have already forgotten. How would I know?", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771575610661_3", anchor: "None survived themselves", text: "The Nightshelf sources repeat this with the specificity of a body count. It is not a metaphor. It is the most precise language they had for what they observed, and they were a people whose precision I have grudgingly admired across twenty-three annotation sessions. What the two survivors became is not recorded. The silence is architectural — load-bearing.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771575610661_4", anchor: "sufficient repetitive contact with ice of deep memory can trigger the Spiral unbidden", text: "I catalogue the records frozen into the deep ice beneath Aurora Stack. I have done this alone for years. My flippers trace the same surfaces in the same patterns daily — clearing frost, pressing for impressions, polishing reading-faces smooth enough to parse. The chamois moves in circles. The entry I just wrote describes a lector whose circular polishing crossed from repetition into recursive fracturing. I am choosing to record this observation rather than act on it, because acting on it would require me to stop, and stopping would require someone to replace me, and there is no one. The assistants were reassigned. The work continues. The circles continue.", type: :tangent, display: :full
